#!/usr/bin/python3
"""litco-supervisor: start and stop per-matter litco-agent slots on a firm host.

A root daemon (FIRM_AGENT_HOST_2026-09-30 section 3.4). The LitKit control
plane calls it over the tailnet to run one matter's turn server as that
matter's own Unix user, from the template unit ``litco-agent@<id>.service``.

API (every route but /health needs ``Authorization: Bearer <supervisor secret>``)::

    PUT  /slots/{id}       create user m_<id> and home /srv/litco/m/<id> (0700) if
                           absent, enable linger, write /run/litco-agent/<id>.env
                           (root 0600), start litco-agent@<id>
    POST /slots/{id}/stop  drain and stop the unit, then delete the env file (202;
                           runs in the background; GET /slots shows the progress)
    GET  /slots            ids, matters, ports and states; never a secret
    GET  /health           liveness; needs no secret and reports none

The slot id is the matter's short id: lowercase letters and digits, at most 30
characters, so ``m_<id>`` fits Linux's 32-character user names. Each slot keeps
the port 8800 + index for its lifetime, recorded in
/var/lib/litco-supervisor/slots.json with no secret in it.

The PUT body carries the matter's secrets (host secret, agent token, model
key, and the TypeSafe key when the firm has one). They go to one place only, the slot's env file on tmpfs, which systemd
reads for the unit's EnvironmentFile=. No response, log line or state file
repeats them. The supervisor's own secret is read from its env file, never
from the process environment, so the commands it runs do not inherit it.

Standard library only; runs under the system python3.
"""

